WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:07.560
Picture a very nervous
seventeen-year-old Julia. My heart was

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.759
pounding my mouth was dry and my hands
were sweaty because I was about to walk

00:00:11.759 --> 00:00:16.980
into my boss's office and ask for my
first pay raise. Somehow I managed to

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.820
negotiate my rate from $7.25 an hour to
$7.75

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.449
10 years later when I found myself in a
similar position you'd think I might

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:32.700
have felt a bit older and wiser going in
but I didn't. Negotiating your pay isn't

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.180
something you get a lot of practice
doing. And it's not like they teach it in

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.690
high school or college and most people
don't even like to talk about how much

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:45.059
they make with anyone even their boss. A
study in Australia found that only 48%

00:00:45.059 --> 00:00:50.039
of men and 32% of women thought their
pay was even up for discussion.

00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:53.699
Your income is the most powerful tool that
you have to grow your financial

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.540
stability so you owe it to yourself to
exert some control over it.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.820
The first step in getting a pay raise is
to decide to ask for one. In a perfect

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:16.000
world your boss would notice all your
hard work and say "Hey that employees a

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.480
real benefit to this company. I should
increase their salary and I'll give them

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.680
a company car and a corner office while
I'm at it!" But this just doesn't happen

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.970
in the real world. Your boss might even
recognize your value but he or she

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.870
probably has their own budget to worry
about so they're unlikely to increase

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:34.590
their expense
without you initiating the conversation.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:39.880
However before you do that you've got to
prepare your case. How are you especially

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.510
valuable to the company? What
achievements or successes have you had

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:47.470
recently? Have your responsibilities
expanded beyond the original job

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:51.910
description? Any extra training or
education relevant to your work? It's

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:55.450
best not to compare your pay to other
employees that's not considered

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.980
professional. And don't include personal
needs like my rent went up or my dog got

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:04.750
sick. Employers will only care about your
individual merit ... and remember that just

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:08.260
doing your job
is not a good enough argument. After all

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:12.010
you already agreed to do that when you
first took the position. You have to show

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:16.180
that you're more valuable now than you
were when you were hired. Although you're

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.650
arming yourself with arguments keep in
mind that it's not a battle. Replacing an

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:24.730
established employee is very expensive
and time-consuming so if you're a good

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:28.900
worker your boss probably wants the same
thing you do; for you to be happy enough

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.590
to stick around. Unless they're an
abusive jerk in which case would getting

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:36.100
a raise really make you happier? Maybe
you should be looking for a different

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:40.630
job instead. Have a specific number in
mind. Researchers at Columbia Business

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:44.410
School found that workers were more
likely to get a raise if they asked for

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.489
a precise amount probably because they
seemed more confident about their worth.

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:52.810
But you do have to be realistic. Most pay raises are between one and five

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.920
percent and you can use websites like a
pay scale or Glassdoor to find out the

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:02.709
market rate for your position in your
area. Timing is key. If the company is in

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:06.340
the middle of a big project and your
boss is stressed out you might want to

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:09.370
wait for things to calm down before
asking for a raise.

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:13.060
plan your request soon after you've
completed a big achievement or shown

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.500
that you can successfully handle more
responsibilities. And if you've recently

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.610
taken a vacation or missed a deadline
you should probably allow some time to

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:25.140
re-establish a reliability. Asking at the
right time will only make it easier for

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:29.340
your boss to say yes. Even if your
employer thinks you deserve it they just

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.360
might not have the ability to increase
your salary which is why you should be

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.640
ready with alternate options. Salary only
typically accounts for about 70% of

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.989
employees total compensation which
leaves lots of room on the negotiating

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:47.400
table for other benefits and perks. And
you can get creative! There's gym

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.209
membership reimbursement, professional
development funding, commuting expense

00:03:51.209 --> 00:03:56.580
coverage more flexible work hours. Heck
maybe it's just a cubicle that's as far

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:01.620
as possible from the obnoxiously loud
co-worker. Come up with the top three

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:05.310
things you would change about your job
and see if they're amenable. When you've

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.480
got your presentation planned out it's
time to rehearse. The thought of

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.080
explaining why you deserve more money
makes most people want to crawl under a

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.579
rock but it's important that you appear
confident in your self-assessment. So

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:20.970
have a friend or family member sit down
with you and just get some

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:24.690
stumble-throughs out of the way. The
mirror isn't gonna cut it here. You need

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:28.440
to sit across from a human being who can
ask you questions that your employer

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.130
might bring up during your talk and
don't ask for feedback from your

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:35.729
rehearsal partner until you've gone
through it at least a couple times.

00:04:35.729 --> 00:04:40.740
And lastly be prepared to hear "NO". Even if
you walk out of your boss's office with

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.240
nothing more than what you went in with
it's not a defeat. You have successfully

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.740
communicated your expectations about
your career and shown your investment in

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:54.120
the company long-term. Trust me your
employer will remember these things the

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.140
next time you're up for review or a
promotion becomes available. You'll also

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.830
be giving yourself practice at asking
for what you want something that is

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:06.270
difficult for a surprising number of
people but essential to feeling like you

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:10.710
have control over your life. As my
mother-in-law always says if you don't

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:15.180
ask the answer is always "No". So there's
no harm in trying if you do it with

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:19.470
preparation and professionalism. And you
won't have to wonder whether you could

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.640
be making more than you are.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.640
And that's our two cents!

